data:{"id":"14cb2da7-bc1f-47c4-b508-f7e1647aabaa","response_type":"tool_result","content":"","done":false,"data":{"ambiguous_slugs":{"concept/open-source-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/unix-like-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/linux-mint":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"]},"duration_ms":3,"error":"","found_kbs":{"concept/multi-tasking-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/network-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/open-source-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/unix-like-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/chrome-os":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/freebsd":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/linux":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/linux-mint":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/ubuntu":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/unix":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"summary/d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"summary/ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"]},"output":"\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[entity/ubuntu|Ubuntu]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003eentity\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux-mint]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0]] (This document compares popular non-Windows operating systems, highlighting Linux distributions like Ubuntu and Mint, and noting the similarities between FreeBSD and Linux despite kernel differences.), [[concept/open-source-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux-mint]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nUbuntu is a popular Linux distribution recommended for users seeking a non-Windows operating system for daily use on personal computers.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Ubuntu\n\nUbuntu is a popular [[entity/linux|Linux]] distribution. It is recommended for users seeking to install a non-Windows operating system on their personal computer for daily use. Along with [[entity/linux-mint|Linux Mint]], it stands out as one of the most popular choices within the Linux ecosystem.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[entity/linux-mint|Linux Mint]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003eentity\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003eMint\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/ubuntu]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/open-source-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/ubuntu]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nLinux Mint is a popular Linux distribution recommended for personal computers as a user-friendly, stable, and secure alternative to proprietary operating systems.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# [[entity/linux|Linux]] Mint\n\nLinux Mint is a widely used Linux distribution cited as a top choice for personal computer installations. Along with [[entity/ubuntu|Ubuntu]], it is identified as one of the most popular distributions within the Linux ecosystem.\n\nThese systems provide a robust alternative to proprietary operating systems, offering stability, security, and a wide range of software support. Their popularity stems from user-friendly interfaces and extensive community support, making them ideal candidates for those transitioning away from Windows.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[concept/multi-tasking-operating-system|Multi-tasking Operating System]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003econcept\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/unix]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/unix]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nA multi-tasking operating system enables the concurrent execution of multiple processes or tasks, a capability found in systems like UNIX and Linux.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Multi-tasking Operating System\n\n**Multi-tasking Operating System**: An operating system capability allowing multiple tasks or processes to be executed concurrently on a computer.\n\n[[entity/unix|UNIX]] is a multi-tasking command-line operating system implemented on many different types of computers. Because it functions as both an independent operating system and a network operating system, UNIX is sometimes referred to as a multi-purpose operating system. [[entity/linux|Linux]] is a popular, free, multi-tasking UNIX-type operating system.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[concept/network-operating-system|Network Operating System]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003econcept\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003eNOS\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/unix]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/unix]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nA Network Operating System supports workstations, personal computers, and terminals on a local area network. UNIX is cited as an example that functions as both an independent and network operating system.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Network Operating System\n\nA Network Operating System is an operating system designed to support workstations, personal computers, and older terminals connected on a local area network.\n\n[[entity/unix|UNIX]] is a multi-tasking command-line operating system implemented on many different types of computers. Because it functions as both an independent operating system and a network operating system, UNIX is referred to as a multi-purpose operating system.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[concept/open-source-operating-system|Open-source operating system]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003econcept\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e开源操作系统\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/unix-like-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/ubuntu]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux-mint]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/freebsd]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/chrome-os]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nThis page defines open-source operating systems as platforms with publicly accessible source code, highlighting Linux distributions like Ubuntu and Mint, as well as FreeBSD.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Open-source operating system\n\nOpen-source operating systems are software platforms whose source code is publicly accessible.\n\n*   **[[entity/linux|Linux]]**: A [[concept/unix-like-operating-system|Unix-like operating system]] often recommended for users seeking a non-Windows OS for daily PC use. [[entity/ubuntu|Ubuntu]] and [[entity/linux-mint|Linux Mint]] are the most popular distributions within this ecosystem.\n*   **[[entity/freebsd|FreeBSD]]**: An open-source operating system that uses a different kernel compared to Linux. However, it shares much of the same software ecosystem, resulting in a desktop experience very similar to typical Linux distributions.\n*   **[[entity/chrome-os|Chrome OS]]**: Also mentioned as an alternative operating system in the broader landscape of non-Windows platforms.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[concept/unix-like-operating-system|Unix-like operating system]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003econcept\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e类 Unix 操作系统\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/freebsd]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/open-source-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nA Unix-like operating system is a category of systems behaving similarly to Unix, including Linux and FreeBSD, which share similar software ecosystems despite kernel differences.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Unix-like operating system\n\nA Unix-like operating system is a category of operating systems that behave similarly to Unix, including [[entity/linux|Linux]] and [[entity/freebsd|FreeBSD]].\n\nLinux is a Unix-like operating system. Other open-source operating systems include FreeBSD. FreeBSD uses a different kernel, but it uses most of the same software found in typical Linux distributions. The experience of using FreeBSD on a desktop PC is very similar to that of Linux.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e","success":true,"tool_call_id":"call_28035c55805e4c4c8501c8ac","tool_name":"wiki_read_page"}}

