data:{"id":"14cb2da7-bc1f-47c4-b508-f7e1647aabaa","response_type":"tool_result","content":"","done":false,"data":{"ambiguous_slugs":{"concept/command-line-interface":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"]},"duration_ms":4,"error":"","found_kbs":{"concept/command-line-interface":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581","fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/graphical-user-interface":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/multi-tasking-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"concept/network-operating-system":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/dos":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/linux":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/mac-os":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/macintosh":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"entity/unix":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"],"summary/ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"]},"output":"\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[entity/unix|UNIX]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003eentity\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[concept/multi-tasking-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/command-line-interface]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/network-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[summary/ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/network-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/multi-tasking-operating-system]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[concept/command-line-interface]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nUNIX is a multi-tasking, command-line operating system that functions as both an independent and network OS across various hardware architectures.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# UNIX\n\nUNIX is a robust operating system designed to function across a wide variety of computer hardware architectures. It is fundamentally characterized as a [[concept/multi-tasking-operating-system|Multi-tasking Operating System]], allowing it to handle multiple processes simultaneously. Its primary interface for user interaction and system management is through a [[concept/command-line-interface|Command-line Interface]] (CLI), which provides powerful control over system resources and operations.\n\nThe unique architecture of UNIX allows it to serve dual roles in computing infrastructure. It operates effectively as a standalone, independent operating system for individual machines, while also possessing the capabilities of a [[concept/network-operating-system|Network Operating System]] (NOS). This dual nature enables it to manage network resources, facilitate communication between different systems, and support distributed computing environments. Due to this flexibility, UNIX is frequently referred to as a \"multi-purpose operating system.\"\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[concept/graphical-user-interface|Graphical User Interface]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003econcept\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003eGUI\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e(none)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[entity/mac-os]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/macintosh]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"95bc02d7-d130-4725-8e15-97455995ba58\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nA visual interface allowing users to interact with electronic devices through graphical icons and visual indicators, contrasting with text-based command-line systems.\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Graphical User Interface\n\nA Graphical User Interface (GUI) is a visual way for users to interact with electronic devices using windows, icons, and menus instead of text-based commands. Unlike command-line interfaces that require users to type text-based commands, the GUI allows users to interact through graphical icons and visual indicators. This approach significantly lowers the barrier to entry for new users and enhances productivity for experienced users by making interactions more intuitive.\n\nThe interface utilizes several key visual metaphors:\n\n*   **Windows**: Rectangular areas on the screen that display the content of applications or files.\n*   **Icons**: Small pictures that represent files, folders, applications, or other objects.\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e\n\n\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[concept/command-line-interface|命令行界面]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003econcept\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003eCLI\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e[[entity/dos]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/unix]] (summary omitted, already seen), [[entity/linux]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e[[entity/unix]] (summary omitted, already seen)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"9286e345-411e-42bf-8083-02c97dac28b2\"/\u003e\n\u003csource knowledge_id=\"ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c\"/\u003e\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\n命令行界面是一种基于文本的用户交互方式，用户通过在提示符下输入特定命令来执行程序和管理文件，区别于图形用户界面。\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# 命令行界面\n\n**命令行界面**（Command-Line Interface，简称 CLI）是一种基于文本的用户界面方法。在此环境中，用户通过在提示符下键入命令来执行程序和管理文件，而不是使用图形元素（如图标、窗口或鼠标交互）。\n\n## 核心特征\n\n*   **文本交互**：界面完全依赖文本输出和输入，没有可点击的菜单或按钮。\n*   **命令提示符**：系统通过命令提示符（例如 `c:\\\u003e`）指示当前驱动器、目录以及准备接收输入的状态。\n*   **手动指令**：所有操作（如启动应用程序、导航目录或管理文件）都必须通过手动输入特定的指令来完成。\n*   **精确语法**：用户需要记忆命令结构和文件路径。例如，要启动字处理程序，用户需输入类似 `wp\\wp.exe` 的命令，其中指定了子目录和可执行文件名。\n\n## 应用背景\n\n命令行界面是早期个人计算机操作系统（如 [[entity/dos|DOS]]）的主要交互模式，也是 [[entity/unix|UNIX]] 和 [[entity/linux|Linux]] 等系统进行系统管理和资源控制的核心接口。虽然现代操作系统多采用图形用户界面（GUI），但命令行界面因其直接和强大的控制能力，在计算历史和高级系统管理中仍占据重要地位。\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e","success":true,"tool_call_id":"call_70486eab65b4457c9f7c1f0e","tool_name":"wiki_read_page"}}

